--- title: Migrating from svelte-motion to Svelte Motion date: '2026-08-23T12:00:00-04:00'
description: Move from the dormant svelte-motion package to @humanspeak/svelte-motion for Svelte 5
runes, AnimatePresence, gestures, drag, layout animations, and SSR-safe SvelteKit support. tags: -
svelte-motion - migration - svelte-5 - animation author: Jason Kummerl readingTime: 7 ---

<BlogPostV2 {post} config={docsConfig}>
    The unscoped `svelte-motion` package has not released since February 2024. It still has users,
    name recognition, and a familiar component model, but it predates Svelte 5's runes-first
    ecosystem. `@humanspeak/svelte-motion` is an actively maintained alternative built specifically
    for Svelte 5. It tracks the modern Motion API and includes `AnimatePresence`, gestures, drag,
    FLIP layout animation, shared-layout transitions, springs, scroll-linked values, and SSR-safe
    SvelteKit rendering. This is a source migration rather than a package alias. The concepts
    transfer cleanly, but imports and component names need to change. ## Replace the package Remove
    the old dependency and install the scoped package: ```bash pnpm remove svelte-motion pnpm add
    @humanspeak/svelte-motion ``` Then replace generic `Motion` imports with either the familiar
    proxy API or tree-shakeable named components: ```svelte
    <script lang="ts">
        // Before
        // import { Motion } from 'svelte-motion'

        // After: familiar proxy syntax
        import { motion } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'

        // Or import a tree-shakeable named component:
        // import { MotionDiv } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'
    </script>

    <motion.div
        initial={{ opacity: 0, y: 12 }}
        animate={{ opacity: 1, y: 0 }}
        transition={{ type: 'spring', stiffness: 320, damping: 24 }}
    >
        Migrated
    </motion.div>
    ``` Use `motion.div`, `motion.button`, and the other element proxies when API familiarity matters
    most. Use `MotionDiv`, `MotionButton`, and the named exports when you want the smallest tree-shaken
    surface. ## Map the animation concepts Most of the animation intent transfers directly: | `svelte-motion`
    concept | `@humanspeak/svelte-motion` equivalent | |---|---| | `Motion` | `motion.div` or `MotionDiv`
    | | `initial`, `animate`, `transition` | The same prop names and semantics | | `exit` | `exit` inside
    `AnimatePresence` | | Variants | Variants with parent-to-child propagation | | Spring and tween transitions
    | Spring and tween transitions | | Drag | Drag with constraints, momentum, elastic, and axis locking
    | | Layout animation | `layout` and shared `layoutId` transitions | The largest practical difference
    is that the new package exposes the modern Framer Motion-style surface across Svelte 5 components,
    gestures, hooks, and layout behavior. The [API reference](/docs/api-reference) lists the complete
    public surface. ## Migrate conditional exit animations Elements cannot animate after Svelte removes
    them from the DOM unless something owns their presence lifecycle. Wrap conditional content in `AnimatePresence`:
    ```svelte
    <script lang="ts">
        import { AnimatePresence, motion } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'

        let open = $state(true)
    </script>

    <button onclick={() => (open = !open)}>Toggle</button>

    <AnimatePresence>
        {#if open}
            <motion.div
                initial={{ opacity: 0, y: 12 }}
                animate={{ opacity: 1, y: 0 }}
                exit={{ opacity: 0, y: -12 }}
            >
                Content
            </motion.div>
        {/if}
    </AnimatePresence>
    ``` Use `mode="wait"` when the next element must wait for the previous one to exit. Use `mode="popLayout"`
    when exiting items should leave document flow immediately. The [live AnimatePresence example](/examples/animate-presence)
    shows the modes in action. ## Replace manual gesture state If the old component used event handlers
    to coordinate hover, press, focus, or viewport state, move that intent into gesture props: ```svelte
    <motion.button
        whileHover={{ scale: 1.05 }}
        whileTap={{ scale: 0.96 }}
        whileFocus={{ boxShadow: '0 0 0 3px #a78bfa' }}
        transition={{ type: 'spring', stiffness: 400, damping: 22 }}
    >
        Continue
    </motion.button>
    ``` This keeps application state focused on application behavior while Motion owns transient animation
    state. Try the [hover and tap example](/examples/hover-and-tap) and [focus example](/examples/while-focus).
    ## Use layout animation instead of measuring boxes Add `layout` when an element should animate between
    its old and new position or size: ```svelte
    {#each items as item (item.id)}
        <motion.li layout>{item.label}</motion.li>
    {/each}
    ``` Use a matching `layoutId` on elements in different UI states to create a shared-element transition.
    The library measures the boxes and applies a FLIP transform automatically. See [reordering](/examples/reordering)
    and [shared layout animation](/examples/shared-layout-animation) for live versions. ## Move state
    to Svelte 5 runes Keep application state in `$state`, compute animation targets with `$derived`, and
    use Motion values for data that updates continuously outside ordinary component state. - `useMotionValue`
    stores an animatable value without forcing component rerenders. - `useSpring` follows another value
    with spring physics. - `useTransform` maps an input range into another value. - `useScroll` supplies
    page or element scroll progress. The [motion values guide](/docs/motion-values) explains when to use
    runes and when a Motion value is the better tool. The [scroll progress example](/examples/scroll-progress)
    combines these APIs in a small, production-shaped example. ## Verify the migration Before removing
    the old package from your lockfile: 1. Search the project for every import from `svelte-motion`. 2.
    Verify conditional elements finish their exit animations before removal. 3. Test drag constraints
    and layout transitions at responsive breakpoints. 4. Load animated routes directly in SvelteKit to
    exercise SSR and hydration. 5. Test keyboard focus and the operating system's reduced-motion preference.
    6. Check any variants that rely on parent-to-child propagation. The package requires Svelte 5 and
    does not provide a Svelte 4 compatibility layer. That sharper baseline is what makes the runes-native
    API, SSR behavior, and current Motion parity possible. Start with the [getting-started guide](/docs),
    then browse the [live examples](/examples) for the specific animation patterns used by your application.
</BlogPostV2>
